  "image_name": "04dca73f10f2c26a460cdaa8a4a59a33.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how nuclear receptor–dependent gene regulation is controlled by chromatin state, metabolic cues, and coregulator exchange. It contrasts a repressed situation on the left with an activated one on the right.\n\nIn the closed-chromatin state, enhancer and promoter regions are inaccessible, and nuclear receptors are associated with corepressors such as NCoR, along with chromatin-remodeling factors that maintain repression. This corresponds to low or blocked target-gene expression. Signals from ligands and metabolism feed into the system, influencing the receptor and partner factors.\n\nA chromatin-priming step then appears to open the enhancer region. In the open state, the receptor and other transcription factors can bind more effectively, including at hormone response elements (HREs). The figure highlights assisted loading, cooperativity, and HRE binding as mechanisms that help stabilize receptor occupancy. Additional transcription factors and post-translational modifications seem to promote this active configuration.\n\nA key transition is a coregulator shift from metabolic corepressors to metabolic coactivators, shown by replacement of NCoR with NCoA. Once coactivators are recruited, mediator, general transcription factors, and RNA polymerase II assemble at the promoter, leading to transcriptional activation of the target gene. Overall, the image depicts gene control as a dynamic switch integrating chromatin remodeling with metabolic signaling.",
